    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>The equation makes no sense.</b> It does if you respect operator order. <b>13 × 3 × 9 − 2 + 9</b>: multiplication first, so 351, then subtract 2 and add 9. The answer is <b>358</b>. If you added before multiplying you will land on a different three-digit number and the lock will not take it. There is no display on the lock, so there is no error message — it simply does not release.</p>
    <p><b>You cannot find 8563.</b> It is not written on a wall. It is on the second <b>Piece of paper</b>, and that paper is tied to the nutcracker beat at 2:12. If your bag has no second paper, you have not done whatever the nutcracker does. Check the inventory for the nutcracker first — if it is there and the paper is not, you are closer than you think.</p>
    <p><b>The safe is a different lock from the first one.</b> Two locks, and they look alike at a glance: both are dark metal boxes with dial columns. The difference is the count — three columns versus four. 358 goes in the three-column lock; 8563 goes in the four-column one. Trying 358 on the safe, or 8563 on the padlock, wastes the most time in this room.</p>
    <p><b>The fruit bowl does nothing when you tap it.</b> Correct — it is a display, not a container. The bowl's symbols and the blue projection are clues, and we could not establish which lock they feed, so do not expect tapping the bowl to open anything.</p>
    <p><b>The cards do not open anything.</b> Also correct, as far as we can tell. They go into the bag and stay there. If Chapter 3 later asks you for card values, you have them; in this room they are a collectible, not a key.</p>

    <h2>Where Dining Room sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and Dining Room is room <b>1 of 15</b> — the first room of the chapter, and the first room of the game that takes place in a house rather than a business. The chapter's rooms are the rooms of a domestic interior: dining room, living room, study room, guest toilet, workshop, guest bedroom, master bathroom, kids playroom, backyard, garden, kitchen, driveway, kids bedroom, storage room, garage. If you have finished Chapter 2 you will notice the change immediately: no tills, no shop fronts, no lift, and a much smaller footprint per room.</p>
    <p>Dining Room sets the template the rest of the chapter uses: one furnished room, two or three code locks, and clue props that are read rather than manipulated. The two locks here are the two kinds the chapter repeats — an arithmetic lock whose clue is printed on it, and a safe whose code comes off a found item. If you want to know what comes next, Living Room is room 2 and it is the same shape of problem.</p>

